{
 "id": "ElectricityC02",
 "slug": "electricity/c02",
 "chapter": "The premise and its rules",
 "topic": "What if electricity disappeared forever?",
 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "rules: not a blackout",
   "text": "Here are the rules. This isn't a blackout. Electricity itself has stopped working. No current flows through any wire, anywhere on Earth."
  },
  {
   "beat": "how you know",
   "text": "How do you even know it's forever? The first morning, everyone waits. Power workers walk the lines on foot. Nothing is broken. The wires are fine. The power plants are fine. Electricity just isn't there anymore."
  },
  {
   "beat": "rules: nothing stored",
   "text": "No batteries. No generators. No solar panels. Not even the tiny battery in your watch."
  },
  {
   "beat": "what's lost",
   "text": "Every photo you ever took. Every message. Every saved game. All of it is locked inside machines that will never turn on again."
  },
  {
   "beat": "rules: what still works",
   "text": "But some things still work. Fire works. Anything with no electric parts works. Bikes. Sailboats. Old steam trains. Your grandpa's wind-up clock."
  },
  {
   "beat": "the weird part",
   "text": "And here's the weird part. Your brain and your heart run on tiny electrical signals. Those still work. Otherwise this would be a very short video."
  },
  {
   "beat": "the rule",
   "text": "So the rule is simple. Anything people built to carry electricity is dead. Every wire, every chip, every screen. Silent. Forever."
  },
  {
   "beat": "plant",
   "text": "Remember that rule. Because one night, far from home, you're going to see it broken."
  }
 ],
